    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1517</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> writes his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ninety-five-theses">Ninety-five Theses</a> against the sale of indulgences</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1521</span><span class="rf-text">Luther refuses to recant at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diet-of-worms">Diet of Worms</a> and is outlawed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edict-of-worms">Edict of Worms</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1525</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-peasants-war">German Peasants’ War</a> is crushed; the first adult baptisms in Zurich begin the Anabaptist movement</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1534</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-supremacy-1534">Act of Supremacy</a> makes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a> supreme head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1541</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-calvin">John Calvin</a> returns to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>, which adopts his Ecclesiastical Ordinances</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1555</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-augsburg">Peace of Augsburg</a> lets each prince of the empire choose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> for his lands</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1598</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iv-of-france">Henry IV of France</a> grants the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a> toleration in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edict-of-nantes">Edict of Nantes</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1648</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> ends the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years’ War</a> and recognises the Reformed faith in the empire</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/albert-of-brandenburg"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/albert-of-brandenburg.jpg" alt="Albert of Brandenburg" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/albert-of-brandenburg">Albert of Brandenburg</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cardinal_Albrecht_of_Brandenburg_(DE_SPSG_GKI10219).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lucas Cranach the Elder, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>At the beginning of the 16th century the Church of western and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> was a single institution under the pope in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, with its own law, courts and taxes, and it accompanied every stage of life from baptism to burial. Lay piety was flourishing. People joined religious brotherhoods, went on pilgrimage, paid for masses for the souls of the dead and obtained indulgences: grants by the Church that remitted the temporal punishment still owed, in this life or in purgatory, for sins already forgiven, offered in return for prayers, pilgrimages or gifts of money. There was also much criticism of the clergy: of poorly educated priests, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> who held several dioceses at once and seldom lived in them, and of popes deeply involved in the wars and politics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Earlier movements of dissent had been suppressed or contained. The followers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-wycliffe">John Wycliffe</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> were driven underground, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-hus">Jan Hus</a> was burned as a heretic at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-constance">Council of Constance</a> in 1415, although his followers kept a church of their own in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>.</p><p>Two developments of the 15th century gave would-be reformers new means. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">Printing</a> with movable metal type, developed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-gutenberg">Johannes Gutenberg</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> around 1450, made books and pamphlets cheap and quick to produce. Humanist scholars called for a return to the sources of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, the Bible in its original languages and the early Church Fathers; in 1516 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erasmus">Erasmus</a> published the Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> with notes correcting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> Vulgate. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, a patchwork of principalities, church lands and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/free-imperial-city">free cities</a> under an elected emperor, there was long-standing resentment of the money that flowed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> to Rome.</p><p>The occasion of the break was an indulgence. To raise money for the rebuilding of St Peter’s Basilica in Rome, Pope Leo X authorised a campaign of indulgence preaching in the lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-of-brandenburg">Albert of Brandenburg</a>, archbishop of Mainz and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a>, under an agreement that half the proceeds would repay the loan Albert had taken from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fugger-family">Fugger</a> bank to pay the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> for holding his sees. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-order">Dominican friar</a> Johann Tetzel preached it near the borders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> in 1517, and people from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a> crossed the border to buy. There <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a>, an Augustinian friar and professor of theology at the university founded by the elector <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-iii-elector-of-saxony">Frederick the Wise</a> in 1502, saw the campaign as a danger to souls.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aims">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aims</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/marburg-colloquy"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marburg-colloquy.jpg" alt="Marburg Colloquy" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/marburg-colloquy">Marburg Colloquy</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marburger-Religionsgespr%C3%A4ch.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AnonymousUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Luther did not set out to found a new church. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ninety-five-theses">Ninety-five Theses</a>, written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> as propositions for academic debate, questioned the power of indulgences and the way they were sold. As he was challenged, however, he went further. In three treatises of 1520, To the Christian Nobility of the German Nation, On the Babylonian Captivity of the Church and On the Freedom of a Christian, he taught that sinners are made right with God by his grace, received through faith alone and not earned by good works; that scripture, not popes or councils, is the final authority in matters of faith; and that all baptised Christians share in a common priesthood. He reduced the seven sacraments of the medieval Church to those he found instituted by Christ in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a>, chiefly baptism and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eucharist">Lord’s Supper</a>, and called on the German nobility to reform the Church if the clergy would not.</p><p>Other reformers shared these principles but differed on much else. In Zurich <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huldrych-zwingli">Huldrych Zwingli</a> taught that the Lord’s Supper was a memorial of Christ’s sacrifice, while Luther insisted that Christ’s body and blood were truly present in the bread and wine; at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marburg-colloquy">Marburg Colloquy</a> of 1529 they agreed on fourteen articles of faith but not on this one, and the evangelical movement divided into Lutheran and Reformed branches. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-calvin">John Calvin</a>, whose Institutes of the Christian Religion first appeared in 1536, stressed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> of God, including his predestination of those to be saved, and a church governed by pastors and elders that supervised the morals of its members. The radical reformers known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anabaptism">Anabaptists</a> baptised only adult believers, formed congregations separate from the state and, in most of their groups, refused to swear oaths or bear arms.</p><p>New texts gave the churches their shape: Luther’s German Bible and his Small Catechism of 1529, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg-confession">Augsburg Confession</a> drawn up by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-melanchthon">Philip Melanchthon</a> in 1530, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-cranmer">Thomas Cranmer</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-common-prayer">Book of Common Prayer</a> of 1549 and Reformed statements of faith such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg-catechism">Heidelberg Catechism</a> of 1563. Worship moved into the language of the people, with sermons and congregational hymns at its centre; monasteries were dissolved, and priests were allowed to marry. The Catholic Church, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-trent">Council of Trent</a> between 1545 and 1563, reaffirmed the teachings the reformers rejected, among them the seven sacraments, the sacrifice of the Mass and the authority of tradition alongside scripture, while also reforming many of the abuses they had attacked.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="events">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Course of Events</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/diet-of-worms"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/diet-of-worms.jpg" alt="Diet of Worms" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/diet-of-worms">Diet of Worms</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9B%D1%8E%D1%82%D0%B5%D1%80_%D0%B2_%D0%92%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%BC%D1%81%D0%B5.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Anton von Werner, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Luther sent his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ninety-five-theses">Ninety-five Theses</a> to Archbishop Albert on 31 October 1517; the story that he also nailed them to the door of the Castle Church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a> comes from later accounts, and historians debate it. Printed copies spread through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> within weeks. In 1519, in a debate at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> with the theologian Johann Eck, Luther declared that church councils as well as popes could err. In 1520 Pope Leo X threatened him with excommunication, and Luther publicly burned the papal bull. Excommunicated in January 1521, he appeared that April before the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diet-of-worms">Diet of Worms</a> and refused to recant unless proved wrong by scripture and plain reason. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edict-of-worms">Edict of Worms</a> placed him under the ban of the empire, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-iii-elector-of-saxony">Frederick the Wise</a> had him hidden in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wartburg">Wartburg</a>, where he translated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> into German; it was printed in September 1522.</p><p>Reform spread fastest in the towns, where preachers, printers and city councils introduced it, often under pressure from citizens. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huldrych-zwingli">Huldrych Zwingli</a> began preaching through Matthew’s Gospel at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grossmunster">Grossmünster</a> in Zurich in 1519, and from 1523 the city council adopted his reforms; in January 1525 a group of his more radical followers baptised each other as adults, the beginning of the Anabaptist movement. In 1524–1525 peasants and townspeople across southern and central Germany rose against their lords in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-peasants-war">German Peasants’ War</a>, citing the Gospel in their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twelve-articles">Twelve Articles</a>. Luther, who feared that the rising would discredit the Gospel and overturn all order, urged the princes to crush it; as many as 100,000 people were killed, and the radical preacher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-muntzer">Thomas Müntzer</a> was executed after the rebels’ defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-frankenhausen">Battle of Frankenhausen</a> in May 1525.</p><p>Princes and cities now decided the fate of reform. From the mid-1520s rulers such as the elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> and Philip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> organised Lutheran churches in their lands; in 1525 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-duke-of-prussia">Albert of Prussia</a> turned the lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> into a Lutheran duchy, and the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> followed. When the Diet of Speyer in 1529 voted to restore the Edict of Worms, the evangelical estates protested, and in 1530 they presented the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg-confession">Augsburg Confession</a> to the emperor. Charles V, long distracted by wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, defeated their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schmalkaldic-league">Schmalkaldic League</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-muhlberg">Battle of Mühlberg</a> in 1547 but could not restore the old faith, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-augsburg">Peace of Augsburg</a> of 1555 allowed each prince of the empire to choose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> for his lands.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="people">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">People</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/martin-luther.jpg" alt="Martin Luther" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lucas_Cranach_d.%C3%84._-_Martin_Luther,_1528_(Veste_Coburg).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lucas Cranach the Elder, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> (1483–1546), a mining entrepreneur’s son from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisleben">Eisleben</a>, had abandoned the study of law in 1505 for the Augustinian friary at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erfurt">Erfurt</a>. He brought to the movement a deep anxiety about salvation and a gift for forceful German prose, and his pamphlets, sermons, hymns and Bible made him the most widely read author of the early Reformation. His colleague <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-melanchthon">Philip Melanchthon</a>, a humanist professor of Greek, gave Lutheran teaching its systematic form and reorganised schools and universities. In Zurich Zwingli’s work was carried on after his death in battle at Kappel in 1531 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-bullinger">Heinrich Bullinger</a>, and in Strasbourg <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-bucer">Martin Bucer</a> worked to reconcile the Lutheran and Swiss reformers before ending his life in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-calvin">John Calvin</a> (1509–1564), a French lawyer and humanist who had fled persecution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> in 1536, was expelled in 1538 and returned in 1541, when the city adopted his Ecclesiastical Ordinances. With the Consistory of pastors and elders he made Geneva a model Reformed city: it punished moral offences, burned the anti-Trinitarian Michael Servetus in 1553 and from 1559 trained ministers at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/academy-of-geneva">Academy of Geneva</a> under Theodore Beza, Calvin’s successor. From Geneva and other centres the Reformed faith spread to France, the Netherlands, parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-knox">John Knox</a> led the break with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scottish-reformation-parliament">Scottish Reformation Parliament</a> made law in 1560. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a>, seeking the annulment of his marriage to Catherine of Aragon, broke with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-supremacy-1534">Act of Supremacy</a> of 1534 made him supreme head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a>; Archbishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-cranmer">Thomas Cranmer</a> led its turn to Protestant doctrine under Edward VI.</p><p>The Reformation was also the work of thousands of less famous people: preachers and printers, the townspeople who crowded to sermons, and the readers of a flood of pamphlets, some illustrated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a> workshop of Lucas Cranach. Women took part too: Argula von Grumbach, a Bavarian noblewoman, published letters in 1523 defending a young Lutheran teacher, and Katharina Schütz Zell, who in 1523 married the Strasbourg preacher Matthew Zell, published a hymn book for lay people. Anabaptist leaders such as Menno Simons, a former priest from Friesland, gathered communities that survived persecution, later named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mennonites">Mennonites</a> after him. On every side there were martyrs: Protestants burned by Catholic authorities, Catholics executed under Protestant rulers, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anabaptism">Anabaptists</a> put to death by both. The reformers’ Catholic opponents, among them Johann Eck, Thomas More and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ignatius-of-loyola">Ignatius of Loyola</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a>, were as committed as they were.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="outcomes">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Outcomes</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/french-wars-of-religion"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/french-wars-of-religion.jpg" alt="French Wars of Religion" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/french-wars-of-religion">French Wars of Religion</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_masacre_de_San_Bartolom%C3%A9,_por_Fran%C3%A7ois_Dubois.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">François Dubois 1529-1584, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The division of Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> brought a century of conflict. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-wars-of-religion">French Wars of Religion</a>, eight wars between 1562 and 1598, Catholics fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a>, as French Protestants were called, for control of the kingdom; thousands of Huguenots were killed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-bartholomews-day-massacre">St. Bartholomew’s Day Massacre</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and other towns in 1572, and peace came only when Henry IV, a Protestant who had become a Catholic, granted them toleration in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edict-of-nantes">Edict of Nantes</a> in 1598. In the Netherlands religious grievances fed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-revolt">Dutch Revolt</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> from 1568, which produced in the north a republic whose public church was Reformed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> swung from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a>’s break with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to Protestant reform under Edward VI, to Mary I’s restoration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>, under which more than 280 Protestants were burned, and to the Protestant settlement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a>, whose government executed more than a hundred Catholic priests, and some lay people who sheltered them, as traitors. In the empire, rivalry between the confessions helped to bring on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years’ War</a> of 1618–1648, in which an estimated 4.5 to 8 million people died, most of them civilians who fell to plague, hunger and other disease.</p><p>By 1648 the religious map of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> was largely fixed. Lutheran churches held northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>; Reformed churches the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and parts of Germany and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a> stood apart from both; and Anabaptist communities survived as minorities. Catholicism, renewed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-trent">Council of Trent</a> and new orders such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a>, kept southern Europe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, held the loyalty of most of the Irish, and won back much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, Hungary and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> of 1648 recognised the Reformed faith alongside the Catholic and Lutheran in the empire, took 1624 as the normal year for church property and public worship, and gave religious minorities limited rights to worship in private.</p><p>The reformers’ hopes were only partly fulfilled. Luther had hoped for a renewed, united Church; instead Western Christianity was divided for good, and each confession built its own disciplined identity through catechisms, church visitations and courts, a process historians call confessionalisation, which also strengthened rulers’ power over their subjects. Most reformers, Protestant and Catholic, believed a state should have one true faith, and punished dissent; toleration came less from their principles than from the stalemate of war and from local agreements such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edict-of-torda">Edict of Torda</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> in 1568 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw-confederation">Warsaw Confederation</a> of 1573.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/king-james-version"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/king-james-version.jpg" alt="King James Version" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/king-james-version">King James Version</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KJV-King-James-Version-Bible-first-edition-title-page-1611.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Church of England, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Reformation left lasting churches that today count hundreds of millions of members: Lutheran, Reformed and Presbyterian, Anglican, Baptist and Mennonite, and the many later Protestant movements that grew from them, among them the Methodists and Pentecostals. It shaped languages and cultures. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luther-bible">Luther’s Bible</a> helped to form standard written German, and translations such as the Welsh Bible of 1588 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-james-version">King James Version</a> of 1611 became classics of their languages. Its demand that every Christian should know the scriptures encouraged schooling and literacy, and its hymns and chorales began a tradition of church music that led to the work of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-sebastian-bach">Johann Sebastian Bach</a>.</p><p>Its political effects were as far-reaching. Lutheran and Calvinist writers developed arguments for resisting an ungodly ruler that fed later theories of limited government; persecuted groups such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a> and the English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puritans">Puritans</a> carried their faith abroad, the Puritans to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a> from the 1620s; and the long experience of religious war strengthened the case for toleration that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-locke">John Locke</a> and others made later in the 17th century. Max Weber’s essay The Protestant Ethic and the Spirit of Capitalism (1904–1905) linked Protestant, especially Calvinist, discipline to the rise of capitalism, a thesis still debated and widely criticised.</p><p>Historians’ views have changed greatly. Protestant historians long told the story as the liberation of the Gospel from a corrupt Church, and Catholic historians as a revolt that tore Christendom apart. In the 20th century Catholic scholars such as Joseph Lortz came to recognise Luther’s religious seriousness, while studies such as Eamon Duffy’s The Stripping of the Altars (1992) showed how vigorous late medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> had been, and many historians now speak of several Reformations, Protestant and Catholic, rather than one. Luther’s late writings against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, exploited by Nazi propagandists four centuries later, have been repudiated by many Lutheran churches. In 1999 the Lutheran World Federation and the Catholic Church signed a Joint Declaration on the Doctrine of Justification, affirming a shared understanding of the basic truths of the doctrine over which the division had begun.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Protestant Reformation, begun by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a>’s protest against indulgences in 1517, divided Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. Its teaching of salvation by grace through faith and of the authority of scripture created Lutheran, Reformed, Anglican and Anabaptist churches, transformed worship, schooling and language, and became entangled in a century of religious war that ended in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> in 1648.</em></p>

